introduction:
  bigQuestion: "How can machines learn from data, and what responsibilities come with that power?"
  overview: "This unit introduces machine intelligence as a shift from fixed-rule programming to data-driven prediction. You will classify machine-learning approaches, match hardware to deployment needs, and examine ethics in real-world systems."
  journey: "You will describe ML types and applications, describe hardware requirements from laptops to cloud and edge systems, discuss ethical implications such as bias and accountability, and discuss how ethical standards must be reassessed as technologies become embedded in daily life."

concepts:
  - title: "ML type selection"
    icon: "fa-brain"
    content: |
      <p>Machine learning includes multiple approaches with different data and output patterns.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Supervised:</strong> labeled data for prediction.</li>
          <li><strong>Unsupervised:</strong> pattern discovery without labels.</li>
          <li><strong>Reinforcement:</strong> reward-based policy learning.</li>
          <li><strong>Deep and transfer learning:</strong> advanced neural and reuse workflows.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Hardware and deployment"
    icon: "fa-microchip"
    content: |
      <p>Hardware decisions depend on model scale, latency, energy, and cost.</p>
      <ul>
          <li>CPU for flexible small-scale tasks.</li>
          <li>GPU/TPU for parallel model training.</li>
          <li>Edge accelerators for local real-time inference.</li>
          <li>Cloud/HPC for high-demand training workloads.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Ethical implications"
    icon: "fa-scale-balanced"
    content: |
      <p>ML ethics is not optional. It is part of system quality.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Fairness and bias</strong> in data and outputs.</li>
          <li><strong>Accountability</strong> for automated decisions.</li>
          <li><strong>Consent and privacy</strong> in data collection and inference.</li>
          <li><strong>Environmental impact</strong> from large compute usage.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Technology integration"
    icon: "fa-globe"
    content: |
      <p>As AI becomes pervasive, ethics must be revisited continuously.</p>
      <ul>
          <li>AR/VR and personalization systems alter lived experience.</li>
          <li>Data ownership and surveillance boundaries become central policy issues.</li>
          <li>Governance must adapt as capability and risk evolve.</li>
      </ul>

terminology:
  - term: "Narrow AI"
    definition: "Task-specific AI systems used in real deployments today."
  - term: "General AI"
    definition: "Hypothetical AI with broad human-level capability across domains."
  - term: "Machine Learning"
    definition: "Systems that learn predictive patterns from data rather than fixed explicit rules."
  - term: "Supervised / Unsupervised / Reinforcement"
    definition: "Three core learning paradigms based on labels, structure discovery, or reward."
  - term: "Deep Learning"
    definition: "ML using multi-layer neural networks for complex feature extraction."
  - term: "Transfer Learning"
    definition: "Adapting a pre-trained model to a new but related task."
  - term: "GPU / TPU"
    definition: "Parallel-focused processors used for matrix-heavy machine-learning workloads."
  - term: "Edge Deployment"
    definition: "Running model inference locally near data generation for low-latency decisions."
  - term: "Algorithmic Bias"
    definition: "Unfair output patterns caused by skewed data, design choices, or evaluation gaps."
  - term: "Accountability"
    definition: "Clear responsibility for decisions and harms arising from automated systems."

misconceptions:
  - mistake: "AI understands like a human"
    correction: "ML systems detect statistical patterns; they do not reason like human cognition."
  - mistake: "More data automatically means fairer outcomes"
    correction: "Large datasets can still carry structural bias and require active auditing."
  - mistake: "Ethics only means privacy"
    correction: "Ethics also includes fairness, accountability, consent, transparency, and societal impact."

